This research investigates the application of machine learning and deep learning models to forecast Alzheimer's disease based on speech samples. The study utilized the AUDITUS dataset, which is comprised of speech samples from patients with Alzheimer's disease and healthy controls. The dataset was pre-processed through a series of steps including filtering, normalization, and feature extraction. The performance of various machine learning and deep learning models was evaluated using multiple metrics, with the results revealing that neural networks outperformed other models with an accuracy rate of 89%. Furthermore, the study's findings suggest that the proposed system is capable of providing reliable diagnoses, thus highlighting the potential of speech analysis in Alzheimer's disease diagnosis. The study's outcomes have significant implications for the development of speech-based diagnostic tools for Alzheimer's disease. The study's code and results are publicly available on the GitHub platform. The study's findings have significant implications for the development of speech-based diagnostic tools for Alzheimer's disease. The study's outcomes have significant implications for the development of speech-based diagnostic tools for Alzheimer's disease. The study's code and results are publicly available on the GitHub platform. \end{document} 
